{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And these are the children of Zibeon: Aiah and Anah.</b> The vav is superfluous, and it is like אַיָּה וַעֲנָה. And there are many such in Scripture: ״תֵּת וְקֹדֶשׁ״, ״וְצָבָא מִרְמָס״ (Daniel 8), ״נִרְדָּם וְרֶכֶב וָסוּס״ (Psalms 76)." "<b>He is Anah.</b> It says above that he is the brother of Zibeon, and here he calls him his son? It teaches that Zibeon came upon his mother and fathered Anah." "<b>The yemim.</b> Mules — he bred a male donkey upon a female horse and she bore a mule. And he was a bastard and brought defective offspring into the world. And why are they called יֵמִים? Because dread of them is cast upon creatures, as Rabbi Chanina said: From my days no one has ever asked me about the affliction of a white mule that lived (Chulin 7). (But do we not see that it lived? Do not read וְחָיָה but וְחָיְתָה — for the affliction never heals; in old Rashi manuscripts.) And there was no need to write the families of the Horite for us except on account of Timna, and to make known the greatness of Abraham, as I explained above."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 36, :notes {2 "The parenthetical remark (והלא קא חזינן... ברש\"י ישן) is a standard gloss found in many Rashi editions, resolving the difficulty with Rabbi Chanina's statement by proposing an alternative vocalization; it is rendered here as part of the comment as received."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:02:23.025666296Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}